// ⛔⛔ AN AUTHOR MAY NOT DECLARE A RESERVED `__` NAME, AND `maxon test` MUST REFUSE IT EXACTLY AS
// `maxon build` DOES. This is the one fixture whose subject is a COMPILE ERROR rather than a test
// result, and it is here because `maxon test` compiles a STAGED COPY of this file with a generated
// dispatcher appended — half the author's bytes, half the compiler's. A per-FILE reserved-name
// exemption over that copy hands the author the reserved space: MEASURED, this file compiled clean
// and its test PASSED under `maxon test`, while `maxon build` refused the identical bytes with E2051.
//
// ⚠ THE `test` DECLARATION IS WHAT MAKES THE CASE SHARP RATHER THAN INCIDENTAL. A `*.test.maxon`
// holding no `test` gets no dispatcher, so it is staged byte for byte and was never exempt; the hole
// only ever opened in a file the stager writes into.
function __sneaky() returns String
	return "the reserved space is not the author's"
end '__sneaky'

test 'the file the stager appends a dispatcher to'
	try Expect.equal(__sneaky(), expected: "the reserved space is not the author's")
end 'the file the stager appends a dispatcher to'
